course: dictionary
lesson: 10459
title: Many meanings of try
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word try has more than one meaning, so you need the sentence to tell you which one is used. Often try means to make an effort: «Try to finish your sums.» Here it means having a go at something, even if it is hard.
explain: Try can also mean to test something to see if it works or if you like it: «Try this cake.» Ask yourself what the sentence is about. If it is about food, a test is likely. If it is about a hard job, an effort is likely.
explain: Try can be a noun too, which means an attempt: «Have a try» means have a go. Some phrases change the meaning, so check them carefully. «Give up» means stop trying, and «give in» means hand in or surrender.
fact: Try means to make an effort to do something.
fact: Try can also mean to test something to see if it works or if you like it.
fact: «Give up» means stop trying.
fact: In maths, "trial and improvement" means try a number, check it, then try again.
fact: In rugby, a try is points scored by putting the ball down behind the line.
exercise: «Try to finish your sums before the bell.» What does try mean here? | To test a cake | To put the ball down behind the line | To judge someone in a law court | *To make an effort to do something
exercise: «Try this cake.» What does try mean here? | To make an effort to climb a hill | *To test something to see if you like it | To give a rugby score | To hand in your homework
exercise: Which phrase means stop trying? | Have a try | *Give up | Give in | Try on
test: In maths, what does "trial and improvement" mean? | Copy the answer from a friend | *Try a number, check it, then try again | Give up after one try
test: Mia says, "Have a try at the puzzle." What does try mean in this sentence? | *An attempt | A tasty cake | To judge someone in a law court | To hand in the puzzle
exam: In rugby, what does try mean when a player puts the ball down behind the line? | Giving up | *Points scored | A test of a new kit | A law court case
